<p>British Prime Minister Keir Starmer faced a widening Labour Party revolt after Health Secretary Wes Streeting resigned on Thursday, saying he had lost confidence in Starmer’s leadership and calling for a contest over the party’s future. Streeting did not formally trigger a leadership election. His resignation followed Labour’s poor local and regional election results last week, when the party suffered heavy losses and came under pressure from Reform UK. Greater Manchester Mayor Andy Burnham took a step toward a possible challenge after Labour MP Josh Simons said he would resign his Makerfield seat to create a path for Burnham to return to Parliament. Labour’s National Executive Committee then gave Burnham permission to seek selection for the Makerfield by-election, an initial step in a process that could lead to a formal bid. Starmer has said he will fight on. The turmoil also hit financial markets as investors weighed political uncertainty and higher deficit risks.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7INYs5OGVLXjmxopTT8o6pQsSpUJtgUZjSaJVgq4HUUukbM8ZVB_bCOlRvDQYG5zhIC9U8Pr_IoXToRUqWBxsAtA160e3btHB4BsK6KcXstitERBtY1mltFR54buw50Xjw1dsJ15S_e5ICvoRb1eEXuYf0P2c8lcgpee01JYt3YtaFLCgAIC2b1Lp5cezUm8NDvBOYRNJpO9vcysycCDX2_vJX1l5v3kdsl4svpDEDrikzwONFnuKqa6Co7fCn8b4gpuYdwlDzTKqoY9Yg' alt='British Prime Minister Keir Starmer meets police officers to discuss operational planning, in London, Friday, May 15, 2026. (Peter Nicholls/Pool Photo via AP)' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Potential field: Former deputy prime minister Angela Rayner said she could mount a leadership run after resolving issues with her tax affairs.</li><li>Makerfield contest: Labour expects a strong Reform UK challenge in the seat, where Simons had a majority of 5,399 over Nigel Farage’s party at the last general election.</li><li>Cabinet pressure: Four junior ministers resigned before Streeting, and more than 80 Labour MPs urged Starmer to quit while more than 100 lawmakers called for him to stay.</li></ul><blockquote>Where we need vision, we have a vacuum. Where we need direction, we have drift - Wes Streeting</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Wes Streeting: Streeting said Labour’s next phase should be a debate over ideas, not personalities or factional score-settling, and argued that members and trade unions wanted the broadest possible contest. (<a href='https://tribune.com.pk/story/2608214/uk-minister-quits-calls-for-leadership-contest'>The Express Tribune</a>)</li><li>Keir Starmer’s allies: Communities Secretary Steve Reed defended Starmer on the BBC, saying Starmer remained Labour’s elected leader and the country’s elected prime minister and that no challenger had emerged for either role. (<a href='https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/labour-leadership-keir-starmer-uk_uk_6a072cc2e4b0ee7169722c88'>HuffPost UK</a>)</li><li>Josh Simons: Simons said standing aside was difficult because Makerfield was his family’s home and Wigan Infirmary had recently saved his newborn son’s life, but said the decision was a choice to act rather than defend the status quo. (<a href='https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/labour-mp-quits-seat-to-let-andy-burnham-make-mp-comeback-and-launch-labour-leadership-bid_uk_6a05e5afe4b0def65bfc5a8c'>HuffPost UK</a>)</li><li>Nigel Farage and Reform UK: Farage said Reform UK would “throw absolutely everything” at a Makerfield by-election if Burnham stands. (<a href='https://www.thenational.scot/news/26108525.reform-uk-will-throw-everything-by-election-takes-place/'>The National</a>)</li><li>Tim Bale: The Queen Mary University of London politics professor said Labour appeared to have decided Starmer would have to go and be replaced by someone who could connect with the public. (<a href='https://www.independent.com.mt/articles/2026-05-15/world-news/UK-Labour-government-faces-weeks-of-uncertainty-over-the-prime-minister-s-future-6736289757'>The Malta Independent</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.